  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 942 PM CDT Sun Jul 19 2026\n\nEvening analysis showed a warm front stretched from northwest \nto southeast across far northeast NE into southwest IA and slowly\npushing eastward. To the northwest, a broken line of showers \nand even a cluster of severe storms had developed along a wind \nshift across SD into western NE, but had since largely\ndissipated as of 930 PM with slightly drier air aloft and weaker\nshear this direction. Guidance is in pretty good agreement that\nnothing additional will form. However, given plenty of \ninstability to work with and strengthening moisture transport as\nwe go into the overnight, wouldn't completely rule out another \nstorm popping up with potential for it to produce some gusty \nwinds if it does. One other thing of note overnight will be some\nfog potential along the aforementioned warm front, mainly in \nwithin a county or two of the Missouri River. Model soundings \nsuggest winds aloft will be strong enough to keep it somewhat \npatchy, but we did see development along that same boundary last\nnight/this morning.\n\nWith the warm front continuing to push east, the warmest air of the \nyear thus far looks to build in for Monday. Expect highs in the \nupper 90s and lower 100s for much of the area, with latest short \nterm guidance giving a 60 to 100% chance of hitting at least 100 \ndegrees near and west of the Missouri River. In addition, dewpoints \nlook to remain in the 70s across much of the area, yielding heat \nindices in the 105 to 110 range. A Heat Advisory remains in place \nthrough 10 PM for the entire area.\n\nBy the afternoon, attention turns to strong to severe storm chances \nas a cold front starts to work into the area from the north. Latest \nCAMs are in good agreement that we see scattered storms start to \ndevelop along the boundary in northeast NE into west-central IA by 3-\n4 PM. Once again, plenty of instability to work with, but strongest \ndeep layer shear looks to be confined behind the front. That said, \nthere could be just enough overlap right along the boundary to yield \nsome degree of storm organization and a threat for strong to severe \nstorms. The main threat would be damaging downburst winds as storms \ncollapse, with deep inverted V model soundings. Still some questions \non how far south these storms will make it, but latest guidance \nconfines them to areas north of an east-west line through the Omaha \nmetro. A second round of showers and storms is looking possible (20-\n30% chance) overnight as a shortwave trough approaches. These look \nunlikely to be severe with most of the instability scoured out \nbehind the front. \n\n\nBehind the front, we'll see relief from the heat, especially in the \nWednesday-Friday period. Temperatures on Tuesday look to remain \nclose to average for this time of year, topping out in the upper 80s \nto lower 90s, but expect widespread lower to mid 80s Wednesday, and \neven a few spots staying stuck in the 70s on Thursday. Something \nelse to note is that latest HRRR guidance suggests we could see a \nlittle smoke make it to the surface with the subsidence behind the \nfront. At this point, concentrations look like they'll be rather \nlow, but maybe just enough to notice. Otherwise, we'll also see some \nhigher chances for more widespread showers and a few storms \nWednesday night into Thursday and Thursday night into Friday as a \ncouple shortwave troughs slide through with a decent surface low \npassing by to our south (40-70% chance both nights). Severe weather \nchances continue to look minimal (under 5%) with very little \ninstability in place.\n\nNow for some unfortunate news. The upper level ridging that brought \nus the heat looks to edge back eastward for the weekend, with highs \nlooking to get back into upper 80s and 90s. On top of that, long \nterm outlooks favor warmer-than-average temperatures continuing into \nthe first few days of August.",
